{"builder":"Make one pull request that adds a --tsv flag to the CLI and documents it. It must meet both of these acceptance lines:\n1. \"node bin/csv2json.js --tsv reads tab-separated input on stdin and prints the same JSON array that node bin/csv2json.js prints for the same table as CSV\"\n2. \"README.md documents --tsv\"\n\nSteps:\n1. Read bin/csv2json.js, src/parse.js, test/csv.test.js and README.md. parse(text, sep = ',') in src/parse.js already takes a separator argument, so you probably do not need to change the parser.\n2. In bin/csv2json.js, check process.argv for --tsv. When it is present, call parse with '\\t' as the separator. When it is absent, keep the current comma behaviour exactly as it is. Keep reading from stdin (fd 0) and keep the output format the same: JSON.stringify of the array, followed by a newline.\n3. In test/csv.test.js, add a CLI test that runs node bin/csv2json.js --tsv with input 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' and asserts the output is '[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]\\n', the same as the existing CSV cli test. Keep the existing tests passing.\n4. In README.md, document --tsv: add a usage line such as `node bin/csv2json.js --tsv < table.tsv` and a sentence saying it reads tab-separated input.\n5. Run npm test and make sure it passes. Also run printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv and compare it with printf 'a,b\\n1,2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js; the two outputs must be identical.\n6. Commit the changes and open the pull request.","verifier":"At the PR head, run one check for each acceptance line, in this order.\n\nCheck 1. Expect line: \"node bin/csv2json.js --tsv reads tab-separated input on stdin and prints the same JSON array that node bin/csv2json.js prints for the same table as CSV\"\nRun from the repository root, using at least these tables, each written once as TSV and once as CSV:\n- printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv   versus   printf 'a,b\\n1,2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js\n- printf 'name\\tage\\tcity\\nann\\t30\\tparis\\nbob\\t25\\trome\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv   versus   printf 'name,age,city\\nann,30,paris\\nbob,25,rome\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js\nParse each stdout as JSON.\nPASS: for every pair, the --tsv run prints a JSON array, and it equals the array the CSV run prints for the same table.\nFAIL: for any pair, the --tsv run errors, prints something that is not a JSON array, or prints an array that differs from the CSV run's array (for example, when tabs are not split and each row comes out as a single field).\n\nCheck 2. Expect line: \"README.md documents --tsv\"\nRun: grep -n -- '--tsv' README.md, then read the surrounding text.\nPASS: README.md mentions --tsv and says it is for tab-separated input.\nFAIL: README.md does not mention --tsv, or mentions it without saying what it does.\n\nAdvisory (does not affect PASS or FAIL):\n- Run npm test and report whether it passes, and whether a test now covers --tsv.\n- Check that node bin/csv2json.js without the flag still produces the same output as before (printf 'a,b\\n1,2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js should print [{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}] followed by a newline), and note whether the --tsv stdout is byte-identical to the CSV stdout, trailing newline included."}